  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:separation-of-concerns",
  "domain": "infrastructure",
  "definition": "A foundational design principle holding that a system should be decomposed so that each part addresses a single, distinct concern \u2014 one aspect of functionality or one axis of decision-making \u2014 with minimal overlap between parts. Articulated by Dijkstra in 1974, it underlies modularity, layering, encapsulation, and interface design: by isolating concerns behind boundaries, changes to one concern can be made, understood, tested, and reused without cascading through the rest of the system.",
